# Vendored nocterm provenance

Upstream: nocterm 0.9.0 from pub.dev
          (repository: https://github.com/Norbert515/nocterm)
Vendored: copied from spikes/nocterm-wall/vendor/nocterm (the p8 spike fork),
          consumed via dependency_overrides in tui/pubspec.yaml.

## Patches carried in this fork

Every patch is marked with a `PATCHED (garage ...)` comment at the change
site. Any future patch MUST be listed here and carry the same marker, so an
upstream upgrade = re-apply exactly the patches on this list.

1. Input batching — lib/src/binding/terminal_binding.dart
   (marker: "PATCHED (garage spike)")
   Upstream converts multi-event stdin chunks of "printable" chars into a
   synthetic paste (Ctrl+V + ClipboardManager), and Enter's `\n` counted as
   printable — coalesced keystrokes silently became pastes, swallowing Enter
   and overwriting the system clipboard via OSC 52. Patch: `\n`/`\r` are
   Enter, never printable; runs shorter than 4 chars stay individual key
   events. Upstream PR candidate.
   Regression test: tui/test/batching_patch_test.dart

2. Batching test hook — lib/src/binding/terminal_binding.dart
   (marker: "PATCHED (garage, test hook)")
   The batching logic lived in the private instance method
   TerminalBinding._batchCharacterEvents, unreachable from tests without a
   live terminal. The body moved verbatim (patch 1 lines included) into the
   public top-level `debugBatchCharacterEvents`; the instance method now
   delegates to it. No behavior change. Not for upstreaming.

3. Shift+Tab / coalesced 3-byte CSI parsing — lib/src/keyboard/input_parser.dart
   (marker: "PATCHED (garage): shift+tab")
   Upstream matched the 3-byte CSI finals (ESC[Z, ESC[A-D, ESC[H, ESC[F)
   only when the parser buffer was exactly 3 bytes; coalesced stdin reads
   (normal under load) made ESC[Z fall to the unknown-CSI consumer or a
   stuck-buffer staleness wipe, silently dropping Shift+Tab and coalesced
   arrows. Patch: match by prefix (>= 3), sound per ECMA-48 (a final byte
   directly after ESC [ terminates the sequence). Upstream PR candidate.
   Regression test: tui/test/shifttab_regression_test.dart

4. Debug key opt-out — lib/src/binding/terminal_binding.dart
   (marker: "PATCHED (garage): debug key opt-out")
   Upstream hardcodes Ctrl+G as the debug-mode toggle, intercepted in the
   event loop before component dispatch — the app never sees it. Patch:
   public static flag `TerminalBinding.debugKeyEnabled` (default true =
   upstream behavior); when the embedding app sets it false, Ctrl+G passes
   through untouched (garage uses it as the disengage chord). The intercept
   decision moved into the public top-level `debugShouldHandleDebugKey`
   (the instance method delegates to it — same pattern as patch 2) because
   the binding cannot be instantiated headless; the regression test drives
   that function against parser-produced events. Upstream "make it
   configurable" PR candidate.
   Regression test: tui/test/debug_key_optout_test.dart

5. TerminalXterm output-callback leak — lib/src/components/terminal_xterm.dart
   (marker: "PATCHED (garage): output-callback leak"; test hook marker
   "PATCHED (garage, test hook)" in lib/src/process/pty_controller.dart:
   PtyController.debugOutputCallbackCount)
   Upstream registers an anonymous output callback on the controller in
   _setupControllerHandler and can never remove it: it leaks past unmount
   and past controller swaps in didUpdateComponent. A leaked callback's
   setState hits a defunct element (null _element after unmount) and
   throws, which aborts PtyController's callback fan-out loop — every
   callback registered after the stale one is starved, so a live tile
   sharing that controller renders blank forever. Patch: keep a reference
   to the callback, remove it in dispose() and on controller change, and
   guard the closure with a _defunct flag. Upstream PR candidate.
   Regression test: tui/test/terminal_xterm_callback_leak_test.dart

6. Absolute scroll anchor — lib/src/components/terminal_xterm.dart,
   lib/src/third_party/xterm_pure.dart/src/utils/circular_buffer.dart and
   lib/src/third_party/xterm_pure.dart/src/terminal.dart
   (marker: "PATCHED (garage): absolute scroll anchor")
   Upstream TerminalXterm only supports a private RELATIVE _scrollOffset
   (clamped to the buffer end), so live output drags a scrolled view along —
   a frozen history view (spec tui-scrollback) is inexpressible, and there
   is no public API to drive the offset externally at all. Patch:
   TerminalXterm gains `frozenTopLine` (absolute first-visible-line index;
   when non-null the renderer slices the buffer at exactly that line,
   clamped, and suppresses the cursor), `onBufferMetrics` (reports
   totalLines/droppedLines/viewHeight each build so the embedding app can
   compute the anchor), `initialContent` (written to the terminal once
   at initState, for capture-pane history seeding), and `ignoreAltBuffer`
   (Terminal.ignoreAltBuffer: alt-screen switches 47/1047/1049 become
   no-ops so a tmux-attach client — which otherwise lives in the alt
   buffer where history never accumulates — scrolls into main-buffer
   scrollback; the emulator-side equivalent of the smcup@ terminfo
   override). Supporting getter
   IndexAwareCircularBuffer.droppedLines exposes the private
   _absoluteStartIndex (monotonic overflow-trim count) so an absolute
   anchor stays pinned to the same content while the circular buffer trims.
   All anchor math lives app-side in tui/lib/ui/scroll_anchor.dart.
   Upstream "external scroll control" PR candidate.
   Regression test: tui/test/scroll_anchor_test.dart (anchor math over the
   reported metrics; the vendored slice is exercised by the e2e smoke).

7. Modified tilde keys (Shift+PageUp/Down) — lib/src/keyboard/input_parser.dart
   (marker: "PATCHED (garage): modified tilde keys")
   Upstream matches only the UNMODIFIED tilde sequences exactly (`ESC[5~`,
   `ESC[6~`, ...), so xterm's modified encoding `ESC[<code>;<mod>~` —
   Shift+PageUp arrives as `ESC[5;2~` from every mainstream terminal —
   fell through to the unknown-CSI consumer and the key was silently
   dropped before any component could see it — and the vt220 Home/End
   (`ESC[1~`/`ESC[4~`, exactly what tmux sends) were dropped even
   unmodified. Patch: parse the vt220 editing-key family
   `ESC[[1-6](;<mod>)?~` (Home/Insert/Delete/End/PageUp/PageDown) ahead
   of the exact matches, decoding the xterm modifier bits (mod-1:
   1=shift, 2=alt, 4=ctrl); single-digit codes match only when directly
   followed by `;` or `~` so F5..F12 (`ESC[15~`...) still take the
   upstream path. Required by the tui-scrollback spec (Shift+PageUp/Down
   drive the frozen history view; End snaps back to live). Upstream PR
   candidate.
   Regression test: tui/test/modified_tilde_keys_test.dart

8. Tile-size → PTY propagation — lib/src/components/terminal_xterm.dart,
   lib/src/process/pty_controller.dart, lib/src/process/pty_handler.dart and
   lib/src/third_party/xterm_pure.dart/src/terminal.dart
   (marker: "PATCHED (garage): size propagation")
   Upstream dropped tile→PTY sizing at four points, so every tmux attach
   client stayed 80×24 and tmux (window-size latest) wrapped session
   content at 80 cols inside ~68-col tiles:
   (a) _TerminalRenderer rendered a hardcoded 80×24 ("for now") and
       reported that to onSizeChange — the real size never reached
       _updateSize. Patch: derive cols/rows from constraints via
       LayoutBuilder (falling back to 80×24 only for unbounded axes) and
       pad/iterate lines to that width.
   (b) PtyController.resize silently no-ops before the process runs, and
       the embedding app starts controllers after first layout (staggered
       attach) — the one resize that mattered was dropped. Patch:
       _updateSize records the size and applies it in a microtask (it is
       called during build/layout, where the resize → notifyListeners →
       setState chain must not run synchronously); a resize the PTY could
       not take stays dirty and is re-pushed on the controller's
       transition to running (first attach AND restarts, which respawn at
       the start() default size).
   (c) PtyController.restart() calls dispose(), which clears the listener
       list — a reattach restart silently detached every listener, so the
       running-transition re-push (and all other change notifications)
       died after the first attach hiccup. Patch: restart() preserves the
       listeners across the dispose.
   (d) UnixPtyHandler.resize never resized anything: it wrote an in-band
       `ESC[8;rows;colst` report into the child's stdin (tmux consumes
       that only as the answer to its own startup `CSI 18 t` query — any
       other time the bytes leak into the pane as typed junk, observed as
       `;52;84t` fragments in shell prompts) plus `kill -WINCH` at a
       process group whose winsize had never changed. Patch: a REAL
       TIOCSWINSZ — resolve the pty child's tty (`pgrep -P` + `ps -o
       tty=`, cached per process) and `stty rows/columns` it; the kernel
       delivers SIGWINCH itself. A resize issued before the child forks
       retries briefly with the last requested size.
   Companion knob: Terminal.suppressSizeReports /
   TerminalXterm.suppressSizeReports (default false). It MUST stay false
   for tmux-attach tiles: the pty is pipe-backed at attach time, so the
   `CSI 18 t` → `CSI 8;rows;cols t` handshake is how tmux gets its initial
   client size — suppressing it leaves the client sizeless and the attach
   exits. Debug hook: GARAGE_TUI_RESIZELOG=<file> appends resize-path
   events (same spirit as the sanctioned GARAGE_TUI_KEYLOG).
   Upstream "constraint-sized terminal + real pty resize" PR candidate.
   Regression test: tui/test/terminal_xterm_resize_test.dart

9. Engaged cursor — lib/src/components/terminal_xterm.dart
   (marker: "PATCHED (garage): engaged cursor")
   Upstream never painted the inner terminal's cursor: _convertCellStyle
   computed the reverse flip and then dropped it (a stale "reverse is not
   supported in TextStyle" note — nocterm's TextStyle DOES support
   reverse/SGR 7 end-to-end through the canvas and binding flush), the
   multi-style line fallback collapsed the whole line to one style which
   would have erased the single inverted cell anyway, an EMPTY cursor cell
   (codepoint 0 — the common composer end-of-input case) was never styled
   at all, and the cursor line was matched against the view-relative
   buffer.cursorY instead of the absolute buffer line (wrong as soon as
   scrollback exists). nocterm also hides the HOST terminal's real cursor
   while running, so an engaged Claude Code composer showed no caret
   whatsoever. Patch: TerminalXterm gains `showCursor` (default false =
   upstream behavior); when set — and the view is live (frozenTopLine
   null, scrollOffset 0 — patch 6's frozen gate kept) and the application
   has not hidden the cursor via DECTCEM (Terminal.cursorVisibleMode,
   `CSI ?25l/h`) — the renderer paints the cell at
   (buffer.cursorX, buffer.absoluteCursorY) with TextStyle(reverse: true):
   a solid inverse-video block, no blink. A cursor line with multiple
   styles renders its real spans through RichText (per-span styles,
   softWrap off) instead of the upstream single-style collapse; all other
   lines keep the collapse untouched. Content-level inverse
   (CellAttr.inverse) deliberately stays unpainted, exactly as upstream:
   under the line collapse one inverse-attr cell could flip a whole line.
   The embedding app passes engaged && !frozen (tui/lib/ui/tile.dart) so
   an unengaged wall never shows a caret per tile. Upstream "paint the
   cursor" PR candidate.
   Regression test: tui/test/terminal_xterm_cursor_test.dart
